    27	---
    28	{state.status === "retired" ? <StateEvent view={state} /> : <BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
    29	  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/fed-rate/">The federal funds rate</a><span>September 16, 2026</span></div>
    30	  <div class="content">
    31	    <main class="story" id="story-root">
    32	      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="narrative"><a href="/events/fed-rate/">The federal funds rate</a> &middot; September 16, 2026</p>
    33	      <h1 data-layer="fact">{event.title}</h1>
    34	      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}</p>
    35	      <ReadingModes />
    36	
    37	      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact">
    38	        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
    39	          <ol>
    40	            <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
    41	            <li><a href="#what-rates-can-change">What a rate hike can change</a></li>
    42	            <li><a href="#who-feels-it">Who feels it</a></li>
    43	            <li><a href="#projections">The projections</a></li>
    44	            <li><a href="#the-catch">The catch</a></li>
    45	            <li><a href="#political-pressure">The pressure around the decision</a></li>
    46	            <li><a href="#what-happened-next">What happened next</a></li>
    47	            <li><a href="#unknowns">What we do not know yet</a></li>
    48	            <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
    49	          </ol>
    50	        </details>
    51	      </nav>
    52	
    53	      <KpiStrip kpis={event.kpis} />
    54	      <p class="railed rail-note" data-layer="fact">Story updated {event.updated}.</p>
    55	
    56	      <div data-layer="proof" class="event-record-context">
    57	        <p class="sources-line">The proof layer holds the September statement, implementation note, projections and preliminary press-conference transcript; the July statement and minutes; the official rate history; the policy framework and Jackson Hole speech; economic releases; and the coverage checked against them. The CBO page was recovered from a September 16 Internet Archive copy after its direct fetch returned 403. The Wall Street Journal live card is preserved through its Dow Jones Newswires syndication. The Associated Press reconstruction is preserved as registry-extracted text and markdown because its run retained no raw-body receipt.</p>
    58	      </div>
    59	
    60	      <section id="what-happened">
    61	        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The decision</p>
    62	        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
    63	
    64	        <p data-layer="narrative">Financial markets put {decision.preDecisionHikeOdds} percent odds on a quarter-point increase the day before the decision. The new information was how completely the committee had moved: July's {decision.julyVote.forHold}–{decision.julyVote.forIncrease} hold became a {event.kpis[1].value} increase, and {projections.expectingAnotherIncrease} of {projections.participantCount} policymakers expected another increase by year-end.<Cite s="abc-fed-preview-2026-09-16" passage="odds of a quarter-point rate hike stood at 94%" /><Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-07-29" passage="approved the following statement for release by a 9 – 3 vote" /><Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-09-16" passage="approved the following statement for release by a 12 – 0 vote" /><Cite s="reuters-fed-decision-2026-09-16" passage="16 of 18 policymakers anticipate at least one more quarter-percentage-point hike" /></p>
    65	
    66	        <SourcedBlock source="fomc-statement-2026-09-16" kind="record">
    67	          <p>The Federal Open Market Committee raised its target range by one-quarter percentage point to {decision.range.lower}–{decision.range.upper.toFixed(2)} percent, effective September 17.<Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-09-16" passage="raise the target range for the federal funds rate by 1/4 percentage point" /><Cite s="fomc-implementation-2026-09-16" passage="effective September 17, 2026" /></p>
    68	        </SourcedBlock>
    69	
    70	        <p data-layer="narrative">It was the first increase since July 27, 2023.<Cite s="fed-open-market-operations-2026-09-16" passage="September 17 | 25 | 0 | 3.75-4.00" /><Cite s="fed-open-market-operations-2026-09-16" passage="July 27 | 25 | 0 | 5.25-5.50" /></p>
    71	
    72	        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
    73	          <figcaption><strong>The first increase since 2023</strong><span>Upper limit of the target range</span></figcaption>
    74	          <StepChart
    75	            {...ratePath}
    76	            yRange={[3.5, 5.75]}
    77	            annotations={[
    78	              { date: "2024-01-01", value: 5.5, text: "5.50%", accent: false },
    79	              { date: "2026-09-19", value: 4, text: "4.00%", anchor: "end", accent: true },
    80	            ]}
    81	            caption="The upper bound fell in six steps from September 2024 through December 2025, then rose a quarter point effective September 17, 2026."
    82	            ariaLabel="Federal funds target range upper limit from January 2024 through September 19, 2026"
    83	          />
    84	          <p class="chart-vintage" data-layer="proof">Computed from {seriesReceipt.rangeCount.toLocaleString("en-US")} daily DFEDTARU observations saved September 19.</p>
    85	        </figure>
    86	
    87	        <p data-layer="narrative">Seven weeks earlier, most policymakers had wanted more information before acting, though several already favored an increase.<Cite s="fomc-minutes-2026-07-29" passage="most participants supported maintaining the current target range" /><Cite s="fomc-minutes-2026-07-29" passage="Several participants favored an increase of 25 basis points" /></p>
    88	
    89	        <p data-layer="narrative">Warsh had set a public test in late August: inflation had to move toward the Fed's goal “clearly and at sufficient speed.” By September, consumer prices were still 3.4 percent above a year earlier, and gasoline had supplied more than one-third of August's monthly increase.<Cite s="fed-warsh-jackson-hole-2026-08-28" passage="clearly and at sufficient speed" /><Cite s="abc-fed-preview-2026-09-16" passage="Prices rose 3.4% in August compared to a year earlier" /><Cite s="bls-cpi-2026-09-11" passage="gasoline rose 3.9 percent in August, accounting for over one third" /></p>
    90	
    91	        <p data-layer="narrative">Headline economic growth slowed to {growth.secondQuarterGdp} percent from {growth.firstQuarterGdp} percent. Beneath that slowdown, consumer spending and private fixed investment grew {growth.secondQuarterPrivateDomesticSales} percent, a measure of domestic demand.<Cite s="bea-gdp-q2-second-estimate-2026-08-26" passage="Real gross domestic product (GDP) increased at an annual rate of 1.5 percent" /><Cite s="bea-gdp-q2-second-estimate-2026-08-26" passage="the sum of consumer spending and gross private fixed investment, increased 4.2 percent" /></p>
    92	
    93	        <p data-layer="narrative">Warsh gave three reasons for acting: a stronger economy, inflation trends that still failed his test and a changed view of the most and least likely geopolitical outcomes. By September, officials were no longer counting on the Iran-war energy shock being temporary, according to the Associated Press.<Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="three things that have happened in that intermeeting period" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="our judgment about what is the most likely, or least likely of the geopolitical situation has changed" /><Cite s="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" passage="Fed officials are no longer counting on it being a temporary shock" /></p>
    94	
    95	        <p data-layer="narrative">The wording also hardened. July attributed part of the inflation problem to supply shocks in sectors including energy. September dropped that explanation and said tighter policy would support a “timelier return” to 2 percent.<Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-07-29" passage="in part reflecting supply shocks that have driven price increases in certain sectors, including energy" /><Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-09-16" passage="Today's policy action will support a timelier return" /></p>
    96	      </section>
    97	
    98	      <section id="what-rates-can-change">
    99	        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The mechanism</p>
   100	        <h2 data-layer="fact">What a rate hike can change</h2>
   101	
   102	        <p data-layer="narrative">A higher overnight rate makes borrowing more expensive and slows consumer and business spending. That reduces demand across the economy.<Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="slow consumer and business spending by raising the cost of borrowing" /></p>
   103	
   104	        <SourcedBlock source="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" kind="record">
   105	          <p>That tool leaves the price of oil and gasoline untouched. Warsh's concern was the next step: fuel costs feeding transport, other goods and inflation expectations.<Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="We cannot affect any individual price" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="Don't have second and third order effects in the economy" /></p>
   106	        </SourcedBlock>
   107	
   108	        <p data-layer="narrative">Lower oil and gas shipments through the Strait of Hormuz, disrupted refining and Red Sea shipping had already raised energy and transport costs. CBO estimated that energy added 2.3 percentage points to the annualized second-quarter increase in the Fed's preferred household-price measure. Prices outside food and energy were expected to stay elevated longer because the higher transport costs reached other goods and services more slowly.<Cite s="cbo-iran-cost-2026-09-15" passage="reduction in shipments of oil and natural gas through the Strait of Hormuz" /><Cite s="cbo-iran-cost-2026-09-15" passage="added 2.3 percentage points to the annualized rate of inflation" /><Cite s="cbo-iran-cost-2026-09-15" passage="higher energy prices take longer to affect prices of nonenergy goods and services" /></p>
   109	
   110	        <p data-layer="narrative">Interest-rate changes reach spending, jobs and prices with a delay. The energy shock could fade before tighter credit has fully slowed the economy.<Cite s="fomc-longer-run-goals-2025" passage="Monetary policy actions tend to influence economic activity, employment, and prices with a lag" /><Cite s="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" passage="By the time higher borrowing costs started to slow the economy, the gas price shock could be over" /></p>
   111	      </section>
   112	
   113	      <section id="who-feels-it">
   114	        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Borrowers and savers</p>
   115	        <h2 data-layer="fact">Who feels it</h2>
   116	
   117	        <SourcedBlock source="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" kind="checked">
   118	          <p>Credit-card rates can respond quickly because most cards carry variable rates tied to banks' prime rate. Auto loans and adjustable-rate mortgages can also become more expensive, while savings accounts and certificates of deposit may pay more.<Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="Fed's policy changes flow through to credit card rates rapidly" /><Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="interest rates on savings accounts and certificates of deposit are headed higher" /></p>
   119	        </SourcedBlock>
   120	
   121	        <p data-layer="narrative">Fixed mortgage rates tend to follow the 10-year Treasury yield. Existing fixed-rate loans keep the rate in their contract.<Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="Mortgage rates don't necessarily follow what the Fed does" /><Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="many homeowners locked in low mortgage rates" /></p>
   122	
   123	        <p data-layer="narrative">Consumer-finance analyst Matt Schulz expected one quarter-point increase to have a small effect. The burden grows if several increases stack up over time.<Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="single quarter-point rate increase isn't really going to have a huge impact" /><Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="stack a few these on top of each other over time" /></p>
   124	
   125	        <p data-layer="narrative">Warsh said persistent inflation falls especially hard on people who live from paycheck to paycheck without financial assets, home equity or retirement-plan equity. Stable prices protect the buying power of their take-home pay.<Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="people that don't own financial assets" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="they're living off their paycheck" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="deliver real take home pay increases" /></p>
   126	      </section>
   127	
   128	      <section id="projections">
   129	        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The signal beyond the vote</p>
   130	        <h2 data-layer="fact">The projections</h2>
   131	
   132	        <p data-layer="narrative">Sixteen of {projections.participantCount} policymakers expected at least one more quarter-point increase by the end of 2026. Their median year-end rate rose from {projections.june2026Median} percent in June to {projections.september2026Median} percent in September.<Cite s="reuters-fed-decision-2026-09-16" passage="16 of 18 policymakers anticipate at least one more quarter-percentage-point hike" /><Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="Federal funds rate         4.1    4.1" /><Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="June projection          3.8    3.6" /></p>
   133	
   134	        <p data-layer="narrative">Each individual projection gives where one policymaker thought the rate should stand at year-end. Warsh submitted no projection and declined to guide the next decision.<Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="individual participant’s judgment of the midpoint of the appropriate target range" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="I have not offered a projection of" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="My business is to not give forward guidance" /></p>
   135	
   136	        <p data-layer="narrative">Policymakers projected inflation in the Fed's preferred household-price measure at 3.7 percent for 2026, up from 3.6 percent in June, and 2.3 percent for 2027. Their median reaches 2 percent in 2029.<Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="PCE inflation              3.7    2.3   2.1   2.0" /><Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="June projection           3.6    2.3   2.0" /></p>
   137	      </section>
   138	
   139	      <section id="the-catch">
   140	        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
   141	          <p class="tt-label">The catch</p>
   142	          <ol>
   143	            <li>
   144	              <h3>The matching 4.1 percent medians conceal different paths</h3>
   145	              CBS wrote that “policymakers expect to hold rates steady throughout 2027.” Each year's 4.1 percent figure is the middle of a separate set of individual year-end judgments. It does not establish a committee decision to hold at every 2027 meeting.<Cite s="cbs-fed-decision-2026-09-16" passage="policymakers expect to hold rates steady throughout 2027" /><Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="For each period, the median is the middle projection" /><Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="appropriate target level for the federal funds rate at the end of the specified calendar year" />
   146	            </li>
   147	          </ol>
   148	        </aside>
   149	      </section>
   150	
   151	      <section id="political-pressure">
   152	        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The White House and the central bank</p>
   153	        <h2 data-layer="fact">The pressure around the decision</h2>
   154	
   155	        <SourcedBlock source="the-hill-trump-fed-reaction-2026-09-16" kind="checked">
   156	          <p>About two hours after the announcement, President Donald Trump demanded rates of 1 percent or less and called for the Fed to lower them fast.<Cite s="the-hill-trump-fed-reaction-2026-09-16" passage="Interest Rates in the United States should be 1%, or less" /><Cite s="the-hill-trump-fed-reaction-2026-09-16" passage="Trump’s reaction came about two hours" /></p>
   157	        </SourcedBlock>
   158	
   159	        <p data-layer="narrative">Trump said the next morning that he had told Warsh to vote with the board because the result would not change. He called it “a very tough board” and “very hostile.”<Cite s="nyt-dealbook-trump-fed-rates-2026-09-17" passage="I talked to Kevin, and I said, ‘You might as well vote with the board because it’s not going to matter.’" /><Cite s="nyt-dealbook-trump-fed-rates-2026-09-17" passage="a very tough board" /></p>
   160	
   161	        <p data-layer="narrative">Warsh described central-bank independence as “a two-way street” and said trade and fiscal policymakers should stay in their lane.<Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="Independence is a two-way street" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="trade policy and fiscal policy stay in their lane too" /></p>
   162	
   163	        <p data-layer="narrative">The pressure's effect on the vote remains unresolved. All 12 voters backed the increase, and Warsh declined to discuss conversations with Trump.<Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-09-16" passage="12 – 0 vote" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="I don't have anything for you on discussions with the president" /></p>
   164	
   165	        <p data-layer="narrative">The dispute was also about what a supply shock calls for. Before the meeting, Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent argued that central banks traditionally wait for a shock to spread through the economy before raising rates.<Cite s="dow-jones-bessent-rate-hike-2026-08-31" passage="traditionally you don't raise [rates]...unless you see second or third-order effects" /></p>
   166	      </section>
   167	
   168	      <section id="what-happened-next">
   169	        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">September 17 and 18</p>
   170	        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened next</h2>
   171	
   172	        <SourcedBlock source="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" kind="checked">
   173	          <p>The 10-year Treasury yield slipped the next day, a move the Associated Press tied to easing inflation worries. Mortgage rates and other long-term borrowing costs still follow longer-term yields rather than moving directly with the Fed's overnight rate.<Cite s="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" passage="10-year Treasury yield even slipped a bit, a likely sign of reduced inflation worries" /><Cite s="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" passage="The Fed’s rate can influence longer-term costs such as mortgage rates, but the Fed doesn’t directly control them" /></p>
   174	        </SourcedBlock>
   175	
   176	        <p data-layer="narrative">By September 18, market-implied odds of another increase in October had risen to about 58 percent from 42 percent a week earlier. Goldman Sachs expected an October increase, while Bank of America expected increases in October and December. Natixis strategist Jack Janasiewicz instead saw the move as a reversal of the “insurance cuts” from 2025 rather than the start of an aggressive tightening cycle.<Cite s="cnbc-warsh-followup-2026-09-18" passage="near 58% Friday morning" /><Cite s="cnbc-warsh-followup-2026-09-18" passage="Jack Janasiewicz, portfolio manager and lead portfolio strategist" /><Cite s="cnbc-warsh-followup-2026-09-18" passage="removal of the insurance cuts the Fed delivered in the fall of 2025" /></p>
   177	
   178	        <SourcedBlock source="fomc-calendar-2026-09-19" kind="record" detail>
   179	          <DataTable columns={["When", "Record"]} rows={chronologyRows} caption="Decision chronology" />
   180	        </SourcedBlock>
   181	      </section>
   182	
   183	      <section id="unknowns">
   184	        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Open questions</p>
   185	        <h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2>
   186	        <ul data-layer="fact">
   187	          <li>Whether another increase follows this one. The year-end views are individual judgments, and Warsh declined to prejudge the next decision.<Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="individual assessments of appropriate monetary policy" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="ill-prepared to pre-judge those future actions" /></li>
   188	          <li>How long the Iran-war energy shock lasts.<Cite s="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" passage="the gas price shock could be over" /></li>
   189	          <li>What the next inflation and growth reports show. August inflation in the Fed's preferred measure and the annual national-account updates, including GDP revisions, are due September 30.<Cite s="bea-personal-income-outlays-july-2026" passage="Next release: September 30, 2026" /><Cite s="bea-personal-income-outlays-july-2026" passage="annual updates of national, industry, and regional data" /></li>
   190	          <li>What the September deliberations add. The minutes are due {decision.minutesDueLabel}, three weeks after the decision.<Cite s="fomc-calendar-2026-09-19" passage="The minutes of regularly scheduled meetings are released three weeks after the date of the policy decision." /></li>
   191	        </ul>
   192	      </section>
   193	
   194	      <StorySources event={event.slug} />
   195	      <StoryState view={state} supporting />
   196	    </main>
   197	  </div>
   198	</BaseLayout>}
