    57	September 16, 2026              Chairman Warsh’s Press Conference                  PRELIMINARY
    58	
    59	
    60	greater performance, an attitude of optimism is exactly what I heard inside the FOMC these last
    61	
    62	two days.
    63	
    64	         One basic sign of strength is the state of America’s labor markets. The jobless rate
    65	
    66	remains low at around 4.1 percent, and both job openings and weekly hours have been
    67	
    68	increasing. Unemployment claims, on a four-week moving average, are running at levels
    69	
    70	consistent with full employment. So, the labor side of the Fed’s congressional remit is in good
    71	
    72	shape.
    73	
    74	         Yet for more than five years, inflation has been running above target. So, our
    75	
    76	predominant focus is on the price-stability side of our mandate. The plain fact is that inflation is
    77	
    78	too high and has been for too long.
    79	
    80	         This summer’s inflation readings do not tell me that underlying trends have meaningfully
    81	
    82	improved. Based on the most recent CPI and PPI data, the 12-month change in total PCE prices
    83	
    84	likely was around 3.6 percent in August. Core PCE and CPI prices are running at about 3.2
    85	
    86	percent and 2.4 percent respectively. Too many categories are still posting increases above 3
    87	
    88	percent, on both a 6- and 12-month basis.
    89	
    90	         I noted in Jackson Hole that overall commodity prices also bear watching – and over the
    91	
    92	inter-meeting period, the prices of many of these key inputs have risen.
    93	
    94	         Since my first FOMC meeting as Chairman in June, my colleagues and I have been
    95	
    96	unequivocal in our commitment to price stability, and to our 2 percent PCE inflation objective.
    97	
    98	         At our July meeting, we all agreed that inflation remained too high, and we expressed our
    99	
   100	joint readiness to act as circumstances might require. And a good majority of my colleagues and
   101	
   102	I thought the wiser course then would be to await new information in the inter-meeting period.
