   112	September 16, 2026             Chairman Warsh’s Press Conference                    PRELIMINARY
   113	
   114	
   115	       Last month in Wyoming, I expressed my commitment to a monetary policy discipline,
   116	
   117	not to a decision. I defined the standard for action: We must be confident that underlying
   118	
   119	inflation is moving to our objective, clearly and at sufficient speed.
   120	
   121	       Today, the FOMC decided that this standard has not been satisfied. The committee’s
   122	
   123	unanimous vote shows our resolve to achieve price stability on a timelier basis. We aim to
   124	
   125	ensure that credit and financial conditions are consistent over time with our mandate, that
   126	
   127	relative price changes in some sectors of the economy do not broaden, that inflation
   128	
   129	compensation in market prices stays low, and that inflation expectations remain well-anchored.
   130	
   131	       This afternoon you also received the Summary of Economic Projections. It reflects the
   132	
   133	views of my colleagues on the Committee, but—as in June—I have not offered a projection of
   134	
   135	my own. But like in June, I said I would faithfully discharge the summary of their projections,
   136	
   137	so here goes. In the Summary’s median projections, real GDP rises at 2.3 percent this year and
   138	
   139	2.4 percent next year. Total PCE inflation runs at 3.7 percent this year and falls to 2.3 percent
   140	
   141	next year. The unemployment rate holds steady at about 4.1 percent. The median participant
   142	
   143	judges that the appropriate federal funds rate to be 4.1 percent at the end of this year, and to
   144	
   145	remain there next year. Inflation risks are to the upside while labor risks are roughly balanced.
   146	
   147	       In my meetings these last few weeks—in Jackson Hole, in Asheville at the G-20 meeting,
   148	
   149	which the U.S. hosted, and at a central bank conference in Basel—it was evident that most
   150	
   151	advanced economies are facing price pressures. Their central banks are making their own
   152	
   153	judgments, consistent with their own remits. Our decision today reflects our best judgment in
   154	
   155	service to our remit.
