data/sources/fed-rate/coverage/ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17.txt-9-The Fed’s increase “alleviates concerns around the Fed taking sticky inflation seriously,” Oscar Munoz, head of U.S. economic research at TD Securities, said. “And that they’re ready to act, not just talk about it but actually act.”
data/sources/fed-rate/coverage/ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17.txt-10-The Fed increases the short-term rate it controls to slow borrowing and spending and ideally cool inflation. The Fed’s rate can influence longer-term costs such as mortgage rates, but the Fed doesn’t directly control them.
data/sources/fed-rate/coverage/ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17.txt-11-Warsh throws in towel on ‘temporary’ shocks
data/sources/fed-rate/coverage/ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17.txt-12-For months, Fed officials have considered whether higher oil and gas prices from the Iran war would just amount to a temporary hit to inflation. If so, it might not make sense to hike rates: By the time higher borrowing costs started to slow the economy, the gas price shock could be over and inflation would fall back to the Fed’s 2% target on its own.
data/sources/fed-rate/coverage/ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17.txt-13-But with the Iran war now in its seventh month, Fed officials are no longer counting on it being a temporary shock.
data/sources/fed-rate/coverage/ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17.txt:14:At the Fed’s previous meeting July 29, its statement said inflation was elevated “in part reflecting supply shocks that have driven price increases in certain sectors, including energy.”
data/sources/fed-rate/coverage/ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17.txt:15:But its latest statement Wednesday dropped that reference to supply shocks, and instead noted that consumer and business spending “has been resilient.”
data/sources/fed-rate/coverage/ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17.txt-16-And at his news conference Wednesday afternoon, Warsh said, “our judgment about ... the geopolitical situation has changed.”
data/sources/fed-rate/coverage/ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17.txt-17-Gas prices, meanwhile, have continued their steady march higher, reaching $4.44 a gallon Thursday, according to AAA. That’s 38 cents higher than a month ago. Diesel prices have hit record highs at $6.40, which will push up shipping costs for many types of goods.
data/sources/fed-rate/coverage/ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17.txt-18-The Fed thinks the economy can take it
data/sources/fed-rate/coverage/ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17.txt-19-Whenever the Fed increases interest rates, it raises concerns that higher borrowing costs will ultimately weigh down the economy so much that a recession results. Yet Warsh emphasized that the economy is healthy and has continued to grow despite repeated blows from things like higher gas prices, tariffs, and higher interest rates.
data/sources/fed-rate/coverage/ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17.txt-20-“Our decision comes at a time when the American economy appears to be strengthening,” Warsh said. “New hiring, private-sector earnings, business capital investment — each of these markers has improved in recent months and is pointing in a good direction.
