       <section id="what-rates-can-change">
         <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The mechanism</p>
         <h2 data-layer="fact">What a rate hike can change</h2>
 
-        <p data-layer="narrative">The Fed raises the cost of borrowing to slow consumer and business spending, reducing demand.<Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="slow consumer and business spending by raising the cost of borrowing" /></p>
-
-        <p data-layer="narrative">The Associated Press traced the oil and gasoline shock to the war with Iran, which was in its seventh month by the day after the decision.<Cite s="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" passage="with the Iran war now in its seventh month" /></p>
+        <p data-layer="narrative">A higher overnight rate makes borrowing more expensive and slows consumer and business spending. That reduces demand across the economy.<Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="slow consumer and business spending by raising the cost of borrowing" /></p>
 
         <SourcedBlock source="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" kind="record">
-          <p>A rate increase cannot set the price of oil or gasoline. Warsh said the Fed cannot affect an individual price; it can try to stop a relative-price shock from broadening into second- and third-order effects across the economy.<Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="We cannot affect any individual price" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="Don't have second and third order effects in the economy" /></p>
+          <p>That tool leaves the price of oil and gasoline untouched. Warsh's concern was the next step: fuel costs feeding transport, other goods and inflation expectations.<Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="We cannot affect any individual price" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="Don't have second and third order effects in the economy" /></p>
         </SourcedBlock>
 
-        <p data-layer="narrative">The August CPI increase was partly an energy-price event: gasoline supplied more than a third of the monthly rise. Warsh framed the Fed's task as preventing that relative-price shock from producing second- and third-order effects across the economy.<Cite s="bls-cpi-2026-09-11" passage="gasoline rose 3.9 percent in August, accounting for over one third" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="Don't have second and third order effects in the economy" /></p>
+        <p data-layer="narrative">Lower oil and gas shipments through the Strait of Hormuz, disrupted refining and Red Sea shipping had already raised energy and transport costs. CBO estimated that energy added 2.3 percentage points to the annualized second-quarter increase in the Fed's preferred household-price measure. Prices outside food and energy were expected to stay elevated longer because the higher transport costs reached other goods and services more slowly.<Cite s="cbo-iran-cost-2026-09-15" passage="reduction in shipments of oil and natural gas through the Strait of Hormuz" /><Cite s="cbo-iran-cost-2026-09-15" passage="added 2.3 percentage points to the annualized rate of inflation" /><Cite s="cbo-iran-cost-2026-09-15" passage="higher energy prices take longer to affect prices of nonenergy goods and services" /></p>
 
-        <p data-layer="narrative">CBO described the route by which the shock could spread: lower oil and gas shipments through the Strait of Hormuz, disruption to refining and Red Sea shipping, and then higher transport costs across goods and services. It estimated that energy added 2.3 percentage points to the annualized second-quarter PCE inflation rate and that core PCE inflation would stay elevated longer as energy costs passed into nonenergy prices.<Cite s="cbo-iran-cost-2026-09-15" passage="reduction in shipments of oil and natural gas through the Strait of Hormuz" /><Cite s="cbo-iran-cost-2026-09-15" passage="added 2.3 percentage points to the annualized rate of inflation" /><Cite s="cbo-iran-cost-2026-09-15" passage="core PCE inflation will remain elevated longer" /></p>
-
-        <p data-layer="narrative">The effect is delayed. The Fed's strategy statement says monetary policy influences activity, employment and prices with a lag, which creates a risk that an energy shock fades before tighter credit has fully slowed the economy.<Cite s="fomc-longer-run-goals-2025" passage="Monetary policy actions tend to influence economic activity, employment, and prices with a lag" /><Cite s="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" passage="By the time higher borrowing costs started to slow the economy, the gas price shock could be over" /></p>
+        <p data-layer="narrative">Interest-rate changes reach spending, jobs and prices with a delay. The energy shock could fade before tighter credit has fully slowed the economy.<Cite s="fomc-longer-run-goals-2025" passage="Monetary policy actions tend to influence economic activity, employment, and prices with a lag" /><Cite s="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" passage="By the time higher borrowing costs started to slow the economy, the gas price shock could be over" /></p>
       </section>
 
